  "note": "A turn in which the model defines a generator, compiles it, is handed the compiler's own diagnostic, patches the source it names, and compiles again (ADR 0026 §3). The second call is refused `generator_error` by the real `compilers/generative` child — `/` is not in the DSL — and the third is the model acting on the line it was given, which is the whole claim this transcript exists to drive.\n\n**Hand-written**, as `four-refusals.json` and `rate-limited.json` are, and for the same reason: recording a turn costs a paid call to OpenRouter, and CLAUDE.md #7 says nothing calls a paid service without the user's confirmation, which this pull request did not have. The grant of 2026-09-24 was one session, it was spent on `tests/determinism/proposal/transcript.json`, and `CEILING_USD` now equals what that cost, so a live call fails closed by design.\n\n**So this is not evidence that a model writes this DSL.** Whether the default model can is U10, deferred until the DSL existed and still unmeasured (ADR 0023 §7; docs/plan.md, \"The open questions — for the user\"). What it is evidence of is the loop: that the two tools are offered, that a diagnostic crosses back whole with its line and column, that a source is edited by `apply_patch` on its path, and that the whole turn lands as one patch a person applies.\n\nEvery field but `choices` and `usage.prompt_tokens` is copied from the recorded response in `one-answer.json`, so what the SDK parses is the shape a provider really sends: `provider`, `native_finish_reason`, `service_tier`, `moderation` and the rest. `usage.prompt_tokens` is generous on purpose — the loop refuses a response whose token count says the provider dropped the prompt (ADR 0022 §3), and this prompt carries two sources and fourteen tool schemas.",
